@meta
id: 27-05
title: Adding to a list without losing it
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: set-option, find-path
par: 61
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-P, type the file name
open: TODO.md

@brief
Some options hold a comma-separated list: `path`, `wildignore`,
`backupdir`, `complete`, `formatoptions`, `comments`. Vim gave each of
them a sensible value at startup and a plugin may have added to it since.
Assigning with plain `=` throws all of that away.

So lists get their own operators. `+=` appends an item, `-=` removes one,
`^=` puts one at the front.

`path` is the list `:find` searches. Out of the box it is `.,,` — the
current file's directory and the working directory, and nothing below
them. That is why `:find` cannot see anything in `src/`.

Add `**` to `path` so `:find` searches the whole tree, then open
`refund.provider.ts` and change the refund window from 30 days to 14.

`TODO.md` is open so you have somewhere to start from. Leave it alone.

@keys
| `:set path+=**` | append `**` to the list |
| `:set path-=.` | remove an item |
| `:set path^=src` | put an item at the front — searched first |
| `:set path?` | read the list back |
| `:find name` | open a file by name from anywhere in `path` |

@hints
`:find` is not broken. It is looking in exactly two directories, and the
file is not in either of them.
--
`path` needs `**` added to it — added, not assigned. The operator for
that is `+=`.
--
`:set path+=**` then Enter, `:find refund.provider.ts` then Enter, and
the rename is a search and a `cw`.

@solution
    :set path+=**<Enter>
    :find refund.provider.ts<Enter>
    /30<Enter>  cw14<Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Fifty-three keystrokes, half of them the file name.

The habit to take from this is `+=` rather than `=`. Written as
`:set path=.,**` this lesson would also have worked, and it would have
quietly dropped the empty entry that means "the working directory" — a
difference you would notice a week later, on a different file, and not
connect to this.

The same three operators work on every list option, and two of them are
worth committing to memory now because you will want them in your own
config:

    set path+=**                   " :find searches the whole project
    set wildignore+=*/node_modules/*,*/dist/*
    set formatoptions-=o           " o on a comment line stops adding //

`^=` is the odd one out. It prepends, which for `path` means "search here
first" and for `complete` means "offer these matches before the others".
Reach for it when order matters and `+=` when it does not.

Ending on `:wqa` rather than `:wq` is not decoration: two files are open,
and `:wq` with more than one file in the arglist refuses to quit.

